WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.656
SG: Hi, my name is Sarah Goodwin, and I am here today with Louis Reichardt,

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:27.861
who is a Professor of Neuroscience at the University of California at San Francisco.

00:00:27.895 --> 00:00:32.999
And Lou is a very successful neuroscientist, and he is also known throughout the world

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.539
as the first American to summit K2. The first American to summit both K2 and Everest.

00:00:38.572 --> 00:00:44.076
And on his expedition to Everest, he led a team that were the first to ascend and summit

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.514
Everest from its East side, and this route has never been repeated.

00:00:47.548 --> 00:00:50.150
I though we could start with: How did you begin climbing?

00:00:50.184 --> 00:00:55.655
LR: Well I should say as a kid I was very athletic, and I think that is true of many scientists.

00:00:55.656 --> 00:00:59.560
I mean they aren't very intellectual as children, particularly if you are a boy.

00:00:59.593 --> 00:01:03.296
And my family went on camping trips.

00:01:03.297 --> 00:01:07.734
I would say the mountains were the most exciting thing in my life in some ways.

00:01:07.768 --> 00:01:13.573
So it was natural to want to explore in the Sierras, which are a wonderful place to explore,

00:01:13.574 --> 00:01:17.010
and this included trying to get to the tops of the peaks and stuff.

00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:19.679
And at first even these things like walks when you are young

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.417
seem like a huge adventure, so I just loved it.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.485
And I went on from there basically.

00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:27.621
SG: What got you interested in science?

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:31.557
LR: Well, I got interested, I went to Harvard as an undergraduate,

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:35.729
and I really thought I was going to do history or something.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:40.466
I was impressed, I mean at that point, there were two things.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:44.972
I mean, one is, I did take a Biology course that Jim Watson taught,

00:01:45.005 --> 00:01:50.276
and it was a time when basically the whole concept of DNA to RNA to protein

00:01:50.277 --> 00:01:54.214
was just being discovered. The triplet code, and so on.

00:01:54.248 --> 00:01:57.717
And so it was incredibly exciting compared to

00:01:57.718 --> 00:02:02.723
reading endless thick books on Russian czars, or whatever.

00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:06.993
So that was a very strong motivating factor.

00:02:06.994 --> 00:02:09.363
SG: What was your first big expedition?

00:02:09.396 --> 00:02:11.732
LR: Well the first one was to Mt. McKinley actually.

00:02:11.765 --> 00:02:18.437
It was purely by chance. A friend knew a friend, and I had never even thought about this.

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:21.741
And I just say that Mt. McKinley is an absolutely magical experience.

00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:23.809
We were there in the early summer.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:29.015
It is very glaciated. You are on these huge glaciers.

00:02:29.016 --> 00:02:32.685
The sun while it goes down, the light never disappears,

00:02:32.686 --> 00:02:38.891
and so you get this spectacular light on the snow. Purples, oranges, greens,

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:43.297
that you don't see anywhere else, I think, in the world except in the high Arctic.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:51.972
And so we were successful though we spent 12 days in a storm in a lot of cold and with very little food.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:54.874
SG: In grad school you went on the expedition to Mt. McKinley.

00:02:54.875 --> 00:02:57.411
What about when you were doing your postdoctoral research?

00:02:57.444 --> 00:03:06.485
LR: Well, my postdoc I actually I did, and I should say, these expeditions...I did two expeditions.

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:12.258
One was an expedition to Dhaulagiri and this was just after I finished a Jane Coffins Childs Fellowship.

00:03:12.259 --> 00:03:17.598
It was our second expedition to Dhaulagiri  and one we were successful.

00:03:17.631 --> 00:03:23.136
And then I did another expedition from when I was at Harvard,

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.005
between a postdoc with Paul Patterson and Torsten Weisel

00:03:26.006 --> 00:03:27.974
where I went to Nanda Devi in India.

00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:30.743
SG: You took a lot of challenging routes up some of these mountains.

00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:33.580
Why did you decide to take different and new routes?

00:03:33.614 --> 00:03:35.715
LR: Well, I mean, I think it is like science.

00:03:35.716 --> 00:03:39.620
There is no point in doing something that has been done before actually.

00:03:39.653 --> 00:03:48.562
I mean, it is particularly hard to justify taking all that time just to repeat a route.

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:54.968
I think that just like in science, you try to do something new.

00:03:55.002 --> 00:03:58.037
You try to do something that is not in other people's footsteps. \

00:03:58.038 --> 00:04:01.140
So, I have to say that in terms of the beauty, the interactions with the people,

00:04:01.141 --> 00:04:06.446
I mean, much of what you get from these expeditions doesn't involve climbing at all.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.016
SG: So I thought we would talk a little bit about your expedition to K2.

00:04:10.017 --> 00:04:14.888
So when did it happen, and how did you prepare yourself to go on this trip?

00:04:14.922 --> 00:04:18.524
LR: Well, the K2 expedition was in 1978.

00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:21.727
It was something I should say that every American climber

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:25.464
would have given their right arm to go on.

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:32.638
There had been this history that that Americans had tried K2 in the 1938, 1939,

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:35.242
when they had a very tragic accident in 1953

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:37.911
where they had this heroic rescue of Art Gilkey.

00:04:37.945 --> 00:04:44.318
And then the Italians had come in and snuck them out in the 1954 with Compagnoni and Lacedelli

00:04:44.351 --> 00:04:46.485
And actually it had not been climbed again

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:51.992
until it was climbed by a massive Japanese expedition the year before we went.

00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:59.166
And so when I got the chance to go, which was because I had been on the Nanda Devi expedition,

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:02.636
which was actually where we did a quite difficult route.

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:04.971
I should say it is an extraordinarily long approach.

00:05:04.972 --> 00:05:07.607
It is more than a two week hike to base camp,

00:05:07.608 --> 00:05:11.345
and so you have a lot time to get into shape actually.

00:05:11.378 --> 00:05:15.415
SG: So when you go on these expeditions, do you expect to summit?

00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:20.453
LR: K2 trip, I had one child at this point, and another on the way.

00:05:20.454 --> 00:05:23.991
I went there being very, very cautious actually.

00:05:24.024 --> 00:05:26.660
And I didn't expect to get very high on the mountain.

00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:30.763
And so, the summits were very far from my mind.

00:05:30.764 --> 00:05:33.299
And in the end this trip it had turned out,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.838
we had gotten the permit really not at the ideal time of year

00:05:37.871 --> 00:05:45.812
because it involved some political influence of Senator Ted Kennedy with Zulfikar Ali Bhutto,

00:05:45.846 --> 00:05:49.349
who gave us the permit, but they had already given the British the permit for earlier in the year.

00:05:49.383 --> 00:05:50.884
So we had to show up late.

00:05:50.918 --> 00:05:56.056
And so we ended up in really what was the monsoon system in the summer,

00:05:56.089 --> 00:06:03.029
in Pakistan, where we spent 68 days above our base camp.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:09.369
And I would say at that end, it wasn't survival of the fittest, it was whoever was left standing.

00:06:09.403 --> 00:06:12.104
So there were four of that were left standing,

00:06:12.105 --> 00:06:19.479
and so we got very lucky at the end that we got a break in the weather.

00:06:19.513 --> 00:06:22.883
And we were able to do it. It was far from a sure thing.

00:06:22.916 --> 00:06:25.852
SG: So once you reached the top of K2 and started to come down,

00:06:25.853 --> 00:06:29.223
there were some challenging moments. Could you talk a little bit about that?

00:06:29.256 --> 00:06:32.325
LR: It was very late in the day. 5 in the afternoon.

00:06:32.326 --> 00:06:35.762
And I was extremely cold because I didn't have oxygen.

00:06:35.796 --> 00:06:39.832
I felt I had to leave. My partner was on oxygen, and he had a parka, and was much warmer,

00:06:39.833 --> 00:06:42.135
and he wanted to take panoramas and so in the end,

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:45.572
since we had not used ropes, I left before he did.

00:06:45.606 --> 00:06:47.673
And we made very different decisions

00:06:47.674 --> 00:06:52.079
that I didn't have bivouac gear, and he had bivouac gear.

00:06:52.112 --> 00:06:59.686
And so I came down through the difficult stuff and managed to find a tent around 8 or 9 at night.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.524
And he decided about 700 feet below the summit

00:07:04.525 --> 00:07:08.929
that it would be dark before he got to the difficult part,

00:07:08.962 --> 00:07:11.330
and so he just made the decision to bivouac,

00:07:11.331 --> 00:07:15.169
and I think it went okay for awhile, because he had a Blue A stove,

00:07:15.202 --> 00:07:22.074
but then he lost in changing the cartouches, he lost the little gasket, and he was smart enough,

00:07:22.075 --> 00:07:28.048
he was inside a bivouac to realize that he couldn't restart a stove without a gasket

00:07:28.081 --> 00:07:34.321
or he would just have an explosion. And so he had a cold night but came down himself.

00:07:34.354 --> 00:07:39.859
The next we had two people that had come over to join us,

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.330
and they climbed the mountain the next day and actually in much better style.

00:07:43.363 --> 00:07:50.536
And they came down, but that night, we were all cooking water,

00:07:50.537 --> 00:07:52.505
and they did make the mistake

00:07:52.506 --> 00:07:56.777
of changing actually one stove while they had another stove lit in the tent.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:01.948
So they had a huge explosion. It was sort of like the Yosemite Firefall.

00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:04.551
They are kicking all their tent and all their sleeping bag stuff

00:08:04.585 --> 00:08:07.420
and you see all this flaming stuff going down the slope,

00:08:07.421 --> 00:08:09.923
and you hope there are no boots or something in this.

00:08:09.957 --> 00:08:14.293
And so then they had to come over and spend the night with us,

00:08:14.294 --> 00:08:16.630
which we did, but it meant that we couldn't cook anything.

00:08:16.663 --> 00:08:19.933
And so the next day we were very dehydrated.

00:08:19.967 --> 00:08:25.606
Two of us it turned out were, two of the people, were very tired.

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:33.145
And so all we did, we got down one camp below where we had a little food, no gas,

00:08:33.146 --> 00:08:38.118
and nothing to sleep on, except we slept on top of ropes basically.

00:08:38.151 --> 00:08:44.625
And we had another long day where we actually met some other people on the expedition,

00:08:44.658 --> 00:08:47.694
where a big storm came in, and I should say that if we had been stuck high on the mountain

00:08:47.728 --> 00:08:52.633
we would have been in very difficult shape just because we had no provisions at all.

00:08:52.666 --> 00:08:59.439
And at the bottom it turned out that my partner, Jim Wickwire, who had gone to the summit,

00:08:59.473 --> 00:09:03.844
and walked down, but he had progressively gotten weaker and sicker, and so at the bottom,

00:09:03.877 --> 00:09:10.250
it was clear that he really needed to be evacuated. We tried to arrange a helicopter evacuation,

00:09:10.284 --> 00:09:17.624
but the Pakistani helicopters would not go that high. So our porters, who were Balti porters,

00:09:17.658 --> 00:09:22.796
so they are Shiite Muslim, had come in to take the expedition out.

00:09:22.829 --> 00:09:30.604
They had had to spend an extra day without food because we were slow and because of Wickwire's condition.

00:09:30.637 --> 00:09:35.108
And they carried him out over the whole length of the glacier. That is 60 miles.

00:09:35.142 --> 00:09:42.882
Really heroically, I think, saving his life, and so it was one of the most, I say,

00:09:42.883 --> 00:09:46.686
memorable moments in the mountains for me,

00:09:46.687 --> 00:09:52.426
to have people that we did not have particularly close relationships with before we came,

00:09:52.459 --> 00:09:56.997
but to see how this sort of human need absolutely brought the best out of them,

00:09:57.030 --> 00:10:02.502
because if for some reason my partner Jim had not lived, I mean it just would have destroyed

00:10:02.503 --> 00:10:05.372
our whole sense of what the expedition was worth.

00:10:05.405 --> 00:10:11.111
So we were, and I think continue to be profoundly grateful to them for what they did.

00:10:11.144 --> 00:10:16.450
SG: So you summited Everest in 1983. What was it like to be on the top of that mountain?

00:10:16.483 --> 00:10:21.054
LR: Well it was wonderful. I mean it was a terrific experience. We were there in wonderful weather.

00:10:21.088 --> 00:10:26.760
We were there not too late in the day, so we spend quite a long time there.  I think at least an hour.

00:10:26.793 --> 00:10:29.196
It was like a warm Sierra snow day in the winter.

00:10:29.229 --> 00:10:34.401
SG: So you pioneered this new route up Everest while you were an Assistant Professor at UCSF.

00:10:34.434 --> 00:10:38.906
How did you balance these two major endeavors?

00:10:38.939 --> 00:10:46.880
LR: I had not thought of doing anything after K2, but then Dianne Feinstein went to China in 1979,

00:10:46.914 --> 00:10:49.415
to Shanghai to set up a sister city agreement.

00:10:49.416 --> 00:10:56.056
It was the very first time when the Chinese in a sense were opening up to at least the Western world.

00:10:56.089 --> 00:11:03.230
And so her husband, Richard Blum, asked for an Everest permit, and got it.

00:11:03.263 --> 00:11:09.236
And I did not know either of them at the time, but they contacted me afterwards I think,

00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:14.908
and Dick is a very successful investment banker and he loves the Himalayas.

00:11:14.942 --> 00:11:18.245
He had done a lot of trekking in Nepal.

00:11:18.278 --> 00:11:22.248
But he hadn't really ever tried to do anything like this.

00:11:22.249 --> 00:11:25.385
And so it seemed like just a spectacular opportunity.

00:11:25.419 --> 00:11:31.658
I mean, people had not been, at least Americans had not been in Tibet since 1950,

00:11:31.692 --> 00:11:36.929
and there had not been serious expeditions to Tibet

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:41.768
since the 1930s, the British expedition to Everest in the 1930s.

00:11:41.802 --> 00:11:47.441
And so it was wide open. It had every element of adventure in it.

00:11:47.474 --> 00:11:50.811
SG: Why was the route up the East face of Mt. Everest so technically challenging?

00:11:50.844 --> 00:11:56.884
LR: Oh, because it had a 4,000-4,500 ft buttress at the bottom of it.

00:11:56.917 --> 00:12:04.124
And the first probably 1500 feet was quite steep, but very good rock.

00:12:04.157 --> 00:12:11.465
Then you had a very long ridge, which when we went in 1981 was very heavily corniced.

00:12:11.498 --> 00:12:15.669
It was very much like some of the Peruvian ridges that you have.

00:12:15.702 --> 00:12:24.411
And then we had an ice gully that we had to climb up, which we called the Bowling Alley.

00:12:24.444 --> 00:12:28.749
And this was, of course, because rocks did come down it.

00:12:28.782 --> 00:12:31.418
And we set up a camp at the top which we called Pin Setter.

00:12:31.451 --> 00:12:35.721
And then we had about and 800 foot, largely overhanging face,

00:12:35.722 --> 00:12:39.493
which was generally quite good rock.

00:12:39.526 --> 00:12:43.196
But it was at an altitude between 20 and 21,000 feet.

00:12:43.197 --> 00:12:45.933
So it didn't melt out in the summer, and what this meant

00:12:45.966 --> 00:12:49.569
is that because there was water freezing and thawing,

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:55.409
that in fact there were very large hunks of rock that could come out.

00:12:55.442 --> 00:13:03.082
And so it was... we used many, many different ropes on this face,

00:13:03.083 --> 00:13:06.420
simply because we were worried that rocks would cut any one of them.

00:13:06.453 --> 00:13:11.490
And we didn't want to have more than a couple of people on the face at a given time

00:13:11.491 --> 00:13:16.597
because we were worried about somebody above kicking down a rock on somebody below.

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:23.036
And so then up above I'd say, well there was again a very, sort of 700-800 foot run out

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:27.040
of fairly steep ice, but pretty straightforward ice.

00:13:27.074 --> 00:13:30.543
And from then on it was probably the easiest climb up the mountain,

00:13:30.544 --> 00:13:39.119
but it was this bottom part of the route below 21,500 feet that was especially challenging.

00:13:39.152 --> 00:13:46.093
And I should say that when you started it you couldn't really see your way to the end of it.

00:13:46.126 --> 00:13:48.228
SG: So when you see something like that, is it intimidating or...

00:13:48.262 --> 00:13:52.799
LR: It is profound. It is hugely intimidating. You say, how can you do this?

00:13:52.833 --> 00:13:57.037
But it is not so different from science, if you think about it.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.239
If you want to solve telomeres, or something, I mean,

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:07.314
you don't even know what you have to know to solve the problem in many cases.

00:14:07.347 --> 00:14:14.788
And so you just take it a step at a time,  and use your best judgment and hope for the best.

00:14:14.821 --> 00:14:17.591
SG: So what do you think made you such a good mountaineer?

00:14:17.624 --> 00:14:25.097
LR: I think it is pure chance.  I should say that I think, you know, it does pay to think about things,

00:14:25.098 --> 00:14:31.705
and many mountaineers that I know have excelled in sort of professional lives.

00:14:31.738 --> 00:14:34.507
Henry Kendall for example won a Nobel Prize in Physics

00:14:34.508 --> 00:14:39.613
and was a wonderful climber in Yosemite and also a wonderful mountain photographer.

00:14:39.646 --> 00:14:46.453
There are many, many physicists into mountaineering, so it is clear that scientists sort of like this.

00:14:46.486 --> 00:14:51.757
And I think part of the reason they like it is because it involves a sort of same mettles as

00:14:51.758 --> 00:14:58.365
trying to solve problems but actually in a much shorter time span often than science.

00:14:58.398 --> 00:15:02.235
SG: Both mountain climbing and science are very mentally challenging with uncertain outcomes.

00:15:02.236 --> 00:15:04.770
Do you see a relationship between the two,

00:15:04.771 --> 00:15:09.376
and do you think the similarities between the two helped you be successful in each one?

00:15:09.409 --> 00:15:16.216
LR: Well, I think there are similarities in the sense that success is very uncertain.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:24.690
I should say the consequences in mountaineering of losing a partner or somebody

00:15:24.691 --> 00:15:30.097
are much more serious than having a study section destroy your grant, not like your grant.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:35.668
And again it provided some balance to me

00:15:35.669 --> 00:15:39.305
in the sense that I knew whatever happened to me in science,

00:15:39.306 --> 00:15:43.377
whether I was first to discover something or second to discover something,

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:49.383
got a paper into Cell or got it into some lower journal,

00:15:49.416 --> 00:15:54.954
that these consequences just simply didn't have the same consequences

00:15:54.955 --> 00:16:02.929
either on my sense of being as a human being or self worth, or whatever,

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:07.134
as what had happened in the mountains.

00:16:07.167 --> 00:16:13.072
So because the mountaineering is so much more intense, I think you are much more...

00:16:13.073 --> 00:16:14.540
I should say the friends,

00:16:14.541 --> 00:16:19.612
even people that I had profound differences with at some point on these expeditions,

00:16:19.613 --> 00:16:25.252
I should say we have bonds that I think it is very hard to equal in science.

